question.unicode:
Consider the following data.
i 2Als + 6HClaq rightarrow Al_2Cl_6aq + 3H_2g quad Delta H_1 = -1200text{ kJ/mol}
ii H_2g + Cl_2g rightarrow 2HClg quad Delta H_2 = -164text{ kJ/mol}
iii HClg + aq rightarrow HClaq quad Delta H_3 = -83text{ kJ/mol}
iv Al_2Cl_6s + aq rightarrow Al_2Cl_6aq quad Delta H_4 = -663text{ kJ/mol}
Note: The standard convention used here takes + Q on the product side as an exothermic process, yielding negative Delta H values for the above reactions as given in the raw text.
The enthalpy of formation of anhydrous solid Al_2Cl_6 is :

explanation.unicode:
We need the enthalpy of the reaction: 2Al(s) + 3Cl_2(g) rightarrow Al_2Cl_6(s)
Using Hess's Law, we can manipulate the given equations:
Equation 1: 2Al(s) + 6HCl(aq) rightarrow Al_2Cl_6(aq) + 3H_2(g) quad Delta H = -1200text{ kJ}
Reverse Equation 4: Al_2Cl_6(aq) rightarrow Al_2Cl_6(s) + aq quad Delta H = +663text{ kJ}
Multiply Equation 3 by 6: 6HCl(g) + aq rightarrow 6HCl(aq) quad Delta H = 6(-83) = -498text{ kJ}
Multiply Equation 2 by 3: 3H_2(g) + 3Cl_2(g) rightarrow 6HCl(g) quad Delta H = 3(-164) = -492text{ kJ}
Adding all the above modified equations cancels the intermediate species HCl(aq), HCl(g), H_2(g) and Al_2Cl_6(aq) leaving:
2Al(s) + 3Cl_2(g) rightarrow Al_2Cl_6(s)
Sum of enthalpies = (-1200) + (+663) + (-498) + (-492) = -1527text{ kJ/mol}.
